1. In the worktree (/Users/me/.harness/worktrees/NYT-3), make sure there are no uncommitted changes; commit any that belong to the work to \`harness/nyt-3\`.
2. Check that gh can reach the host: \`gh auth status --hostname github.com\`. If it fails, stop and say so in \`post_note\` (the human needs to run \`gh auth login\`).
3. Push the branch: \`git -C /Users/me/.harness/worktrees/NYT-3 push -u origin harness/nyt-3\`. If the push is rejected because \`origin/harness/nyt-3\` has commits you don't, fetch and merge them, then push again. Never force-push.
4. Check for an open pull request first: \`gh pr view harness/nyt-3 --repo github.com/nytimes/web --json url,state\`. When there is one, the push updated it; add a short comment on what changed with \`gh pr comment\`. Otherwise open one: \`gh pr create --repo github.com/nytimes/web --base main --head harness/nyt-3 --title <title> --body-file <file>\`. Use the ticket's title, and build the body from the spec (\`read_spec\`): its Goal, what changed and why, and how it was verified from its Status. Leave out its Open questions unless they're still open. When the repository has a pull request template (\`.github/pull_request_template.md\` or similar), follow it. Open it ready for review, not as a draft, unless the instructions say otherwise.
5. Call \`record_pull_request\` { url } with the pull request's link, whether you opened it or updated it. The ticket is only done once it's recorded.
6. Remove the worktree (\`git -C /Users/me/Sites/nyt worktree remove /Users/me/.harness/worktrees/NYT-3\`), and keep \`harness/nyt-3\`: the pull request needs it.

